==Evolution==
Evolves into '''Moon Disciple Kaworu''' at level 10.

==Strategy/Usage==
Moon Disciple Kaworu functions almost identically to [[Izanami of Dusk (Legend Rare Cat)|Izanami of Eventide]], but with a few notable differences which tend to make him less optimal in comparison.

Most importantly, Kaworu swaps Izanami's [[Savage Blow]] for [[Sage Slayer]] and [[Knockback]]. These are nigh-worthless on a unit such as Kaworu for one specific reason: lack of applicability. He is a nuker first and foremost, which Knockback does not have good synergy with since enemies staggered/killed will make its activation irrelevant. The distinct lack of [[:Category:Traitless Enemies|Traitless]] [[:Category:Sage Enemies|Sages]] makes Sage Slayer almost cosmetic, as its utility even in mixed [[Zero Legends]] stages is non-existent.

Kaworu's Immunities are his main point of interest. [[Immune to Waves|Immunity to Waves]] provides a unique coverage over Izanami's Surge Immunity, since a majority of the most notable Traitless threats use Wave Attacks as offense. They can prevent him from taking unnecessary damage while fighting these enemies from a relatively safe distance, and if a stage places more of an emphasis on your unit's survival, can make him worth using over Izanami. Stages like [[Furious Field]] would benefit more from Kaworu's Wave Immunity, since Izanami can take huge chunks of damage in an instant. Outside of these niche circumstances, Izanami can simply power through most Wave stages through brute force.

[[Immune to Slow|Slow Immunity]] and [[Immune to Explosions|Explosion Immunity]] are generally less favorable for Kaworu over Freeze and Curse Immunity. Slow Immunity is unnecessary thanks to his increased speed making him more consistent to time over [[Croakley]]'s blind spot, and Explosion Immunity equally suffers from lack of applicability, though he can utilize it when dealing with the [[R.Ost|R. Ost]] and [[Bore Jr.|Bore Jrs]] in [[Feathergeddon: Swan Song (Brutal)|Feathergeddon: Swan Song]].

To summarize, he functions well as an Izanami substitute in niche situations, or if the player doesn't own Izanami of Eventide, but otherwise fares worse in almost every scenario thanks to a mismatched combination of abilities and lower average DPS/HP overall. He can be inconsistent in Uncanny Legends thanks to a lack of [[Immune to Curse|Curse Immunity]], and [[Henry]] becomes a problem for him because of no [[Immune to Freeze|Freeze Immunity]]. That said, his Insane Damage still makes him a monster against a majority of his target trait.

